-- Schema version 2 → 3: the prediction primary key stops collapsing real trains.
--
-- ## The defect
-- `PredictionEntity`'s key ended in `eta`, which is the FORMATTED string, and
-- `insertPrediction` is `INSERT OR REPLACE`. Two trains to the same destination
-- on the same platform 40 seconds apart both format as "1 min", so the second
-- had the same key as the first and overwrote it. The rider was shown one train
-- where two were coming, and the board was a row short.
--
-- `SyncPredictionsUseCase` already dedupes on the absolute arrival time
-- (`targetEpochMs`) precisely so the second one survives — its comment says the
-- row is kept "without losing the row from SQL". It was lost from SQL anyway.
-- The Kotlin fix was defeated by the schema.
--
-- Found by `PredictionSyncCharacterizationTest` on AV2-4.1, raised as **Q7**,
-- and pinned for three sessions by a test that asserted the WRONG behaviour on
-- purpose so the fix would have something to flip.
--
-- ## Why this file is four statements and not a rebuild
-- Everything in this table is a CACHE. Departures older than about two minutes
-- are filtered out on read, and FCM or the first REST refresh repopulates the
-- board within seconds of launch — `1.sqm` drops these same rows for exactly
-- that reason and says so. So there is nothing to copy across: drop, recreate
-- with the new key, done.
--
-- A primary key cannot be ALTERed in SQLite, so a change to one is always a
-- table rebuild. This is the cheapest possible version of that.
--
-- ## What a user notices
-- One board reading "no departures yet" for the second or two between launch
-- and the first payload, once, on the launch after the update. That is the
-- whole cost, and it is the same cost `1.sqm` already imposes.
--
-- ## Keep identical to StationlyDatabase.sq
-- `MigrationTest` asserts that `Schema.create` and `Schema.migrate` produce
-- identical `PRAGMA table_info` for every table, and
-- `verifyCommonMainStationlyDatabaseMigration` fails the BUILD when this file
-- and the `.sq` disagree. Change one, change the other, in the same commit.

-- The index is dropped EXPLICITLY, before the table, for the same reason
-- `1.sqm` does it: SQLite would drop it along with the table, and naming it
-- here is what makes the recreate below fail loudly rather than silently
-- colliding if the drop order is ever changed.
DROP INDEX IF EXISTS prediction_lookup;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS PredictionEntity;

CREATE TABLE PredictionEntity (
    stationId TEXT NOT NULL,
    lineId TEXT NOT NULL,
    direction TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '',
    destination TEXT NOT NULL,
    destId TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '',
    viaKey TEXT,
    matchesFilter INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,
    platform TEXT NOT NULL,
    eta TEXT NOT NULL,
    isDue INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    stopLetter TEXT,
    timestamp INTEGER NOT NULL,
    targetEpochMs INTEGER,
    -- The change. See `StationlyDatabase.sq` for why `eta` stays in the key
    -- rather than being swapped out, and for what a null `targetEpochMs` means
    -- to a SQLite unique index.
    PRIMARY KEY (stationId, lineId, direction, destination, platform, eta, targetEpochMs)
);

CREATE INDEX prediction_lookup ON PredictionEntity(stationId, lineId, direction);
